🚀 Week 2 – Day 4 Command Line Arguments & Varargs in Java ☕

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🧠 Introduction

Java provides special features to:

✔ Pass values while running programs
✔ Handle variable number of arguments

👉 These concepts are useful in **real-world applications & interviews 🔥**

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

⚡ 1. Command Line Arguments

Command line arguments are values passed to the program during execution.

---

📌 Syntax

```java id="c1"
public static void main(String[] args)
```

👉 `args` stores command line inputs as strings.

---

📌 Example Program

```java id="c2"
class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        System.out.println("First Argument: " + args[0]);
        System.out.println("Second Argument: " + args[1]);
    }
}
```

---

▶️ Compile & Run

🔹 Compile

```bash id="c3"
javac Demo.java
```

---

🔹 Run

```bash id="c4"
java Demo Java Programming
```

---

📌 Output

```text id="c5"
First Argument: Java
Second Argument: Programming
```

---

🔍 Important Points

✔ Arguments are stored as strings
✔ Passed during execution
✔ Accessed using array indexing

---

⚠️ Common Errors

❌ Accessing invalid index
❌ Forgetting arguments during execution

---

📌 Example Error

```java id="c6"
System.out.println(args[2]);
```

👉 Causes:

```text id="c7"
ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException
```

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

⚡ 2. Varargs in Java

Varargs allow methods to accept **variable number of arguments**.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 Syntax

```java id="v1"
returnType methodName(dataType... variableName)
```

---

📌 Example

```java id="v2"
class Demo {

    static void add(int... numbers) {

        int sum = 0;

        for (int n : numbers) {
            sum += n;
        }

        System.out.println("Sum = " + sum);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        add(10, 20);
        add(1, 2, 3, 4);
        add(5);
    }
}
```

---

📌 Output

```text id="v3"
Sum = 30
Sum = 10
Sum = 5
```

---

🔍 How Varargs Work?

👉 Internally treated as arrays.

---

📌 Equivalent Form

```java id="v4"
static void add(int[] numbers)
```

---

⚠️ Rules of Varargs

✔ Only one varargs parameter allowed
✔ Must be last parameter in method

---

📌 Valid Example

```java id="v5"
static void display(String name, int... marks)
```

---

❌ Invalid Example

```java id="v6"
static void test(int... x, String name)
```

---

🎯 Advantages of Varargs

✔ Flexible method calling
✔ Cleaner code
✔ No need for multiple overloaded methods

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

⚠️ Common Mistakes

❌ Multiple varargs in one method
❌ Varargs not placed last
❌ Confusing arrays with varargs

---

🧠 Real Insight

👉 Varargs are commonly used in:

* Utility methods
* Logging systems
* Framework APIs

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📚 Key Takeaways

✔ Command line arguments pass values during execution
✔ Arguments are stored in `String[] args`
✔ Varargs allow variable number of inputs
✔ Varargs improve flexibility & readability

